{"version":"2026.10.11-r25","id":"Q409473","formula":"Cl₃N","hill":"Cl3N","elements":["N","Cl"],"composition":{"N":1.0,"Cl":3.0},"names":{"en":"Nitrogen trichloride","ru":"Трихлорид азота"},"descriptions":{"en":"Nitrogen trichloride, also known as trichloramine, is the chemical compound with the formula NCl3. This yellow, oily, and explosive liquid is most commonly encountered as a product of chemical reactions between ammonia-derivatives and chlorine (for example, in swimming pools). Alongside monochloramine and dichloramine, trichloramine is responsible for the distinctive 'chlorine smell' associated with swimming pools, where the compound is readily formed as a product from hypochlorous acid reacting with ammonia and other nitrogenous substances in the water, such as urea from urine.","ru":"Трихлори́д азо́та — бинарное неорганическое соединение хлора и азота с формулой NCl3, тяжёлая маслянистая летучая жидкость жёлтого цвета."},"fame":36,"pubchemCid":61437,"mass":120.36,"charge":0,"smiles":"N(Cl)(Cl)Cl","inchikey":"QEHKBHWEUPXBCW-UHFFFAOYSA-N","iupac":null,"classes":[{"qid":"Q113145171","names":{"en":"type of chemical entity","ru":"тип химического объекта","uk":"тип хімічної сутності"}}],"structureInfo":{"kind":"molecule","source":"pubchem","ref":"61437","atoms":4,"license":"PubChem (NIH), public data"},"structure":{"kind":"molecule","atoms":[{"x":0.758,"y":-1.487,"z":0.14,"el":"Cl"},{"x":0.909,"y":1.4,"z":0.14,"el":"Cl"},{"x":-1.667,"y":0.087,"z":0.14,"el":"Cl"},{"x":0.0,"y":0.0,"z":-0.42,"el":"N"}],"bonds":[[0,3,1],[1,3,1],[2,3,1]]},"sources":[{"role":"identity","name":"Wikidata","url":"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q409473","license":"CC0 1.0"},{"role":"description","name":"Wikipedia (en)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nitrogen_trichloride","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"},{"role":"description","name":"Wikipedia (ru)","url":"https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D0%A2%D1%80%D0%B8%D1%85%D0%BB%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%B8%D0%B4_%D0%B0%D0%B7%D0%BE%D1%82%D0%B0","license":"CC BY-SA 